" -H "Authorization: Bearer $UI_KEY" "http://127.0.0.1:4076$url" done curl -s -w '
HTTP %{http_code}
' http://127.0.0.1:4076/v1/chat/completions \ -H "Authorization: Bearer $UI_KEY" -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \ -d '{"model":"gpt-4o-mini","messages":[{"role":"user","content":"reply with the single word dashboard"}],"max_tokens":10}' ``` The session key really does carry the reserved id, which is what makes the lookup unsatisfiable ``` $ psql -tAc 'select team_id from "LiteLLM_VerificationToken" order by created_at desc limit 1' litellm-dashboard ``` ### Before, at commit `f03df1bb42` ``` /v2/team/list?page=1&page_size=100 -> HTTP 404 {"error":{"message":"{'error': "Team doesn't exist in db. Team=litellm-dashboard. Create team via `/team/new` call."}","type":"auth_error","param":"None","code":"404"}} /user/list -> HTTP 404 same 404 /model/info -> HTTP 404 same 404 /v1/chat/completions -> HTTP 404 same 404 control, master key on the same route: master key /v2/team/list -> HTTP 200 ``` The master key passing the same route is why the breakage looked like a UI-only outage. Driving the dashboard in Chrome, the browser itself saw **34 distinct endpoints return 404**, which is every panel on every page | Virtual Keys | Teams | Internal Users | Models | Test Key | | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | | ![](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/yassin-berriai/litellm-pr-media/main/pr36976/before-virtual-keys.png) | ![](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/yassin-berriai/litellm-pr-media/main/pr36976/before-teams.png) | ![](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/yassin-berriai/litellm-pr-media/main/pr36976/before-internal-users.png) | ![](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/yassin-berriai/litellm-pr-media/main/pr36976/before-models.png) | ![](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/yassin-berriai/litellm-pr-media/main/pr36976/before-playground.png) | ### After, at commit `e02f19fdd9` ``` /v2/team/list?page=1&page_size=100 -> HTTP 200 {"teams":[{"team_alias":"data-science","team_id":"3975b193-33a6-4b5f-b893-457334e3e5ae",... /user/list -> HTTP 200 {"users":[{"user_id":"52cac511-0036-432a-ad8c-66f1b4ec5f35",... /model/info -> HTTP 200 {"data":[{"model_name":"gpt-4o-mini",... /v1/chat/completions -> HTTP 200 {"id":"chatcmpl-ECvsm5CHq7bkmRmBAmEviozy4DqNE","model":"gpt-4o-mini",...,"content":"Dashboard"} control, master key on the same route: master key /v2/team/list -> HTTP 200 ``` Same walkthrough, same seeded database, same browser: **0 failed responses**, and the Test Key pane answers with a real completion off the UI session key | Virtual Keys | Teams | Internal Users | Models | Test Key | | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | | ![](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/yassin-berriai/litellm-pr-media/main/pr36976/after-virtual-keys.png) | ![](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/yassin-berriai/litellm-pr-media/main/pr36976/after-teams.png) | ![](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/yassin-berriai/litellm-pr-media/main/pr36976/after-internal-users.png) | ![](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/yassin-berriai/litellm-pr-media/main/pr36976/after-models.png) | ![](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/yassin-berriai/litellm-pr-media/main/pr36976/after-playground.png) | ### The property #36837 added is intact, on the same running proxy A team allowlist still binds ``` team key on gpt-4o-mini (allowed) -> HTTP 200 {"id":"chatcmpl-ECvt9j7UnyoYMaUBzGo55qySSVMyn","model":"gpt-4o-mini",... team key on gpt-4.1 (not allowed) -> HTTP 403 {"error":{"message":"team not allowed to access model. This team can only access models=['gpt-4o-mini']. Tried to access gpt-4.1","type":"team_model_access_denied",... ``` And a team that really is gone is still a hard refusal. `/team/delete` also removes the team's keys, so this drops the team row straight out of Postgres to leave the key orphaned, which is exactly the case #36837 closed ``` orphan-team key, team row present -> HTTP 200 $ psql -c "delete from "LiteLLM_TeamTable" where team_id = 'f80bef0f-...'" DELETE 1 same key 65s later, team row gone -> HTTP 404 {"error":{"message":"{'error': "Team doesn't exist in db. Team=f80bef0f-b35a-4e0b-88f2-6f88720cc319. Create team via `/team/new` call."}","type":"auth_error",... ``` So on one proxy, at one commit, the reserved id is exempt and a genuinely absent team is not ### Why the reserved id alone does not earn the exemption The synthesized team's `models` is empty, and empty reads as every model, so an identity that merely names the reserved team would be widened, not just waved through. Several auth paths take `team_id` straight from data the proxy did not mint: JWT claims, an OAuth2 introspection response, a custom auth handler's return value An earlier revision excluded those by naming them, which cannot be complete by construction. This one asks the other question, where the credential came from. A database-minted session key is already marked as a virtual key. The `EXPERIMENTAL_UI_LOGIN` blob has no key row to be marked by, and a proxy-admin one returns before the virtual-key paths run, so it is marked where it is decrypted with the proxy's own `ui_hash_key`. Both markers are set only by assignment at a boundary the proxy itself validated, and both are stripped from validated input Each half is pinned. Removing either marker from the predicate turns exactly one login mode red, and a test asserts neither marker survives `model_validate`, so a claim or handler return cannot carry one in. Both mint paths were also driven on the live proxy, since only one of them has a key row and the unit tests cannot tell them apart ``` default login, session credential is a sk- key row -> every dashboard route HTTP 200 EXPERIMENTAL_UI_LOGIN, credential is `hkm...`, 780 chars, 0 rows in LiteLLM_VerificationToken -> every dashboard route HTTP 200 same blob login with the decrypt-boundary marker removed -> HTTP 404, the original bug exactly ``` That last line is why the marker exists: gating on the virtual-key marker alone would have left `EXPERIMENTAL_UI_LOGIN` deployments broken in precisely the way this PR set out to fix ### Reserving the id itself The exemption is keyed on provenance, but the id was still assignable. `/team/new` has always refused it, and key creation did not, so a proxy admin could put an ordinary key on the sentinel team. Only an admin can, since a non-admin naming a team with no row is already refused, but the resulting key inherits the exemption and with it the skip of its owner's user-level model check. The pair below is one owner restricted to `gpt-4o-mini`, two keys identical but for the team ``` before reserving the id key on the reserved team gpt-4.1-mini -> HTTP 200 control key, no team gpt-4.1-mini -> HTTP 403 user not allowed to access model. This user can only access models=['gpt-4o-mini'] after reserving the id POST /key/generate team_id=litellm-dashboard as proxy admin -> HTTP 400 reserved for LiteLLM UI dashboard sessions POST /key/update team_id=litellm-dashboard as proxy admin -> HTTP 400 same the Admin UI login, which mints through generate_key_helper_fn -> unaffected, every route HTTP 200 ``` Already-planted keys keep the exemption, since nothing on the row distinguishes them from a UI session; the guard stops the situation arising rather than repairing one that already has ## Type 🐛 Bug Fix ## Caveats (if any) - Reserved-id guard now lives in the auth gate too - The token-derived team is byte-for-byte what this path built before #36837, so which checks it displaces for a UI session is unchanged and not something this PR revisits ### Final Attestation - [x] The tests check the right things, including the edge cases, and regressions in the respective real-world customer use-cases are not possible after this PR
